//Abstract Class

An abstract class is a class that cannot be used to create objects directly.

A class becomes abstract when it has at least one pure virtual function.

class Animal {
public:
    virtual void sound() = 0;
};

Because sound() is pure virtual, Animal is an abstract class.

So:

Animal a;  // ❌

is not allowed.

But a child class can implement the pure virtual function:

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    void sound() override {
        cout << "Bark";
    }
};

Dog d;  // ✅
Why use an abstract class?

It provides a common interface/rule for child classes.

For example, every Animal must have a sound() function, but each animal can implement it differently.

Simple definition:

Abstract class = a class that cannot be instantiated directly and is used as a base class for derived classes.